{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>You shall do no injustice in judgment.</b> This expression is also stated at the end of the portion: ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּ עָוֶל בַּמִּשְׁפָּט בַּמִּדָּה וּבַמִּשְׁקָל וּבַמְּשׂוּרָה״, and it appears that the explanation of both is one and the same: that one should not commit injustice that is in truth and in justice — I mean, that same act is in righteousness and in justice, and the law dictates that it is proper to be so; yet nonetheless the matter is injustice. And how so? This is what the verse explained the matter and said: ״לֹא תִשָּׂא פְנֵי דָל״ — Rashi explained: that you should not say, he is poor and the rich man is obligated to support him, so I will find him innocent in judgment and he will be supported through clean hands. And this matter is in truth proper and obligatory, for surely the rich man is obligated to support him, and the matter is in justice — but nonetheless it is injustice in judgment, for in the word בַּמִּשְׁפָּט it limits the place: that this is not the place of judgment — I mean, in another place this is not injustice, for surely he is obligated to support him and one may take from the hand of the rich man enough for the sustenance of the poor; but in the place of judgment it is injustice, for one should not alter on this account the line of justice; rather, the judgment should go its way, and at another time he will obligate the rich man to support him. And so too, nearby in the matter of weights and measures, you find a person who acts in justice yet nonetheless the matter is injustice. How so? For you find a person who diminishes his measure and sells it more cheaply than others in order that he should have people flocking to him, for they will think that he sells cheaply and they do not know that he diminished the measure. And according to truth he acts justly, for after all he does not rob people at all; but nonetheless it is injustice, since he does so by way of deception and causes that others have no buyers — and any case like it, in which there is an aspect of justice and an aspect of injustice, is all included in the general rule." "And from the fact that it says here ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּ״, it appears to me that this does not speak of perverting judgment, for that is a negative prohibition that involves no action — rather, it contains a warning that he should not permit to himself what he forbids to others, for any judge who extracts money from another through judgment is no judge (Bava Batra 58a), even though according to its plain sense this means even one who burns his own stack, etc. (see Sifrei Deuteronomy 1:17). Nonetheless there is in it an allusion also to the judge, that he must be free of every blemish and injustice, and only afterward may he reprove others — like the story of that man who cut down his own tree leaning into the public domain first at night (Yerushalmi Bava Batra ch. 2 hal. 11). Therefore it is said to the judges: you shall not do the very thing that you decree in judgment, for it is injustice.\nAnd perhaps it is to this that Rashi intended when he explained that the judge who corrupts the judgment is called an oppressor, hated, etc. — the language of corruption indicates that he is not speaking of perverting judgment, but rather of one who corrupts straight judgment; for when he does not uphold what he decrees for others, he corrupts the judgment, for many people raise a challenge against the judgment and do not wish to uphold it, because they hang themselves after the judge. And therefore Rashi brings as proof from the verse ״כִּי תוֹעֲבַת ה׳ וגו׳ כֹּל עֹשֵׂה עָוֶל״, which is stated in the portion of weights (Deuteronomy 25:16), and there it says: ״לֹא יִהְיֶה בְכִיסְךָ אֶבֶן וָאָבֶן גְּדוֹלָה וּקְטַנָּה״ (ibid. 25:13) — and this refers to one who weighs out for others with the small weight and for himself with the large one; and this parallels the judge who permits to himself what he forbids to others. And afterward Rashi brings the verse ״וְלֹא תָבִיא תוֹעֵבָה אֶל בֵּיתֶךָ״ (ibid. 7:26), for the judge who extracts money from another through judgment — he is the one who brings an abomination into his house: what he decrees for others as an abomination, he brings into his own house. And this is a correct and clear allusion, a precious idea." "<b>In righteousness shall you judge your fellow.</b> But is it only his fellow whom he must judge in righteousness, and not someone who is not his fellow? And perhaps from the force of this very question they learned from here: judge every person to the side of merit (Shavuot 30a) — but the wicked man one need not judge to the side of merit; leave him in his presumption of guilt.\nAnd if it is speaking of actual judgment, it appears to me that it gives counsel to the judge who wishes to do as King David did — judgment to this one and charity to that one (Sanhedrin 6b) — he should do so only for his fellow in Torah and mitzvah, and not for someone who has no connection to him, for perhaps they will make a conspiracy between them." "And there are those who say that it was about this that David prayed and said, ״עָשִׂיתִי מִשְׁפָּט וָצֶדֶק בַּל תַּנִּיחֵנִי לְעֹשְׁקָי״ (Psalms 119:121) — they are the makers of conspiracy, whom he called ״עֹשְׁקָי״.\nAnd likewise ״לֹא תֵלֵךְ רָכִיל בְּעַמֶּךָ״ — this means specifically one who acts as your people acts; but it is permitted to speak evil speech about disputants (Yerushalmi Pe'ah ch. 1 hal. 1), and there are those who say it is a mitzvah, so that this one will know to guard himself from him. And if one did not do so, he transgresses ״לֹא תַעֲמֹד עַל דַּם רֵעֶךָ״, which Rashi explained: to see his death when you are able to save him, etc. — for so too is this matter. Therefore these verses were placed in juxtaposition."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:49.394722290Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}